1	//! End-to-end regression tests for the whole-file contract of
2	//! `chainview inspect <DER-FILE>`: every byte of the file must be exactly one
3	//! complete, validly encoded DER certificate.
4	//!
5	//! The field decoders have their own regression files; this one guards the
6	//! boundary around the certificate as a whole:
7	//!
8	//! - a complete, legal certificate still inspects successfully (exit 0, empty
9	//!   stderr, the five fixed fields in order under the existing display
10	//!   rules), including a certificate whose outer or inner lengths use the DER
11	//!   long form and one whose interior legitimately contains zero bytes or
12	//!   newline bytes (e.g. inside the serial or a UTF8String value);
13	//! - bytes appended after an otherwise viewable certificate reject the whole
14	//!   input, whether the append is one zero byte, whitespace, a newline or a
15	//!   second complete certificate: inspect neither trims whitespace nor shows
16	//!   only the first certificate nor merges two concatenated certificates (two
17	//!   identical certificates back to back are still two certificates);
18	//! - the opposite boundary holds too: while the outermost declared length is
19	//!   not satisfied the input is truncated, not partially displayed — no field
20	//!   is printed just because the subject, serial or validity already happened
21	//!   to be readable; an input that ends inside the long-form length bytes
22	//!   fails the same way;
23	//! - these guarantees concern input completeness only: expired or not-yet-
24	//!   valid certificates remain viewable, and inspect still never verifies
25	//!   signatures or trust.
26	//!
27	//! Every case drives the real binary. Success is exit 0 with empty stderr and
28	//! exactly the five fixed output lines; failure is exit 2, completely empty
29	//! stdout and a `chainview: invalid DER certificate: <reason>` stderr whose
30	//! reason says explicitly that bytes follow the certificate (trailing) or that
31	//! the data ends before the certificate does (truncated).
32	
33	use std::fs;
34	use std::path::PathBuf;
35	use std::process::{Command, Output};
36	use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
37	
38	const FIXED_SERIAL: &str = "0E8A4C2F9B17D603";
39	const FIXED_SUBJECT: &str = "CN=example.com";
40	const FIXED_ISSUER: &str = "CN=Test CA";
41	const FIXED_NOT_BEFORE: &str = "2026-01-15T09:30:00Z";
42	const FIXED_NOT_AFTER: &str = "2027-01-15T09:30:00Z";
43	
44	const UNKNOWN_OID: &[u64] = &[1, 2, 3, 4];
45	
46	const CERT_PREFIX: &str = "chainview: invalid DER certificate: ";
